<%# Signed-in landing page: a guide to each navbar destination. Keep this
    in step with layouts/_base_menu_items. Describe what the page does
    today, and leave out anything unfinished. %>
<div class="container welcome">
  <header class="welcome-header">
    <h1><strong>Welcome to Action Plan</strong></h1>
    <p class="lead">
      Action Plan is where an incident management team builds its Incident
      Action Plan, tracks resources from check-in to demob, and keeps everyone
      working from the same information.
    </p>
    <%= link_to 'Go to Incidents', incidents_path, class: 'btn btn-success' %>
  </header>

  <section class="welcome-callout">
    <strong>Where are the rest of the menu links?</strong>
    Everything in Action Plan belongs to an incident, so most links appear in
    the menu bar only after you open one. Until then, you'll just see
    <strong>Incidents</strong> and your name.
  </section>

  <section id="getting-started">
    <h2>Getting started</h2>
    <ol class="welcome-steps">
      <li>
        <strong>Open an incident.</strong>
        Choose one from <strong>Incidents</strong>, or click
        <strong>New Incident</strong> to create one. If you create it, you're
        its Owner.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Bring in your team.</strong>
        On the incident's page, enter an email address under
        <strong>Collaborators</strong> and click <strong>Invite</strong>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Add your resources.</strong>
        Import IROC requests on <strong>Requests</strong> and check resources
        in from there, or add them directly on <strong>Resources</strong>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Organize them.</strong>
        On <strong>T Cards</strong>, drag each resource into its division or
        group.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Build the plan.</strong>
        Under <strong>IAP</strong>, create a plan for the operational period
        and work through the forms. Each 204 picks up its resources from the
        T Cards board.
      </li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section id="good-to-know">
    <h2>Good to know</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Click to edit.</strong> Most fields on the IAP forms and
        resource tables can be edited by clicking them, and they save
        automatically.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Double-click for detail.</strong> Double-click a T-card or a
        checked-in row on the ICS-211 to see everything about that resource.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Work side by side.</strong> When a teammate changes something
        on your incident, a notice appears with a <strong>Refresh</strong>
        button. Browser tabs are titled by page, so several tabs are easy to
        tell apart.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Guide to the menu</h2>
    <nav class="welcome-toc" aria-label="Guide contents">
      <a href="#guide-incidents">Incidents</a>
      <a href="#guide-iap">IAP</a>
      <a href="#guide-resources">Resources</a>
      <a href="#guide-ops">Ops</a>
      <a href="#guide-plans">Plans</a>
      <a href="#guide-requests">Requests</a>
      <a href="#guide-tcards">T Cards</a>
      <a href="#guide-schedule">Schedule</a>
      <a href="#guide-demobs">Demobs</a>
      <a href="#guide-feed">Feed</a>
      <a href="#guide-help">Help &amp; profile</a>
      <% if current_user.admin? %>
        <a href="#guide-admin">Admin</a>
      <% end %>
    </nav>
  </section>

  <section id="guide-incidents" class="welcome-page">
    <h3>Incidents</h3>
    <p>Lists the incidents you own or collaborate on. Older incidents are under <strong>Previous years</strong>.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>New Incident</strong> starts an incident with its name, type, complexity, location and start date.</li>
      <li>The incident's page holds its information and map, financial codes, and <strong>Collaborators</strong>. You can also pull in details with <strong>Update from IRWIN</strong>.</li>
      <li>Only the incident's Owner or a site admin can delete it.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="guide-iap" class="welcome-page">
    <h3>IAP</h3>
    <p>Build the Incident Action Plan for each operational period. The link opens your most recent plan.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Date tabs switch between plans, and <strong>New</strong> starts the next one. A new plan carries over the previous plan's forms, so you only update what changed. Safety Message hazards carry over, but the message text doesn't.</li>
      <li>
        The form tabs are:
        <strong>Cover</strong>, <strong>202</strong> objectives,
        <strong>203</strong> organization, <strong>204</strong> assignments,
        <strong>205</strong> radio plan, <strong>205A</strong> contact list,
        <strong>206</strong> medical plan, <strong>220</strong> air operations
        and <strong>Safety Message</strong>.
      </li>
      <li>To add a 204, click <strong>+</strong> on the 204 tab and pick a division or group. Use <strong>SELECT CHANNELS</strong> to bring in frequencies from the 205.</li>
      <li>The 205A is shared across the whole incident rather than kept per plan.</li>
      <li>Each form prints on its own with its Print button.</li>
      <li><strong>Publish IAP</strong> locks in the resource list on every 204. Until then, 204s follow the T Cards board. Other form fields stay editable after publishing, and <strong>Unpublish</strong> returns the 204s to the live board.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="guide-resources" class="welcome-page">
    <h3>Resources</h3>
    <p>The master list of everything on the incident.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>ICS-211</strong>: the check-in list, split into Checked In, Released and On R&amp;R. A red LWD means the resource is past its last work day. Click a caret to see a roster, and click <strong>DMB</strong> to start a demob.</li>
      <li><strong>Glide Path</strong>: a 14-day view of when resources time out, printable and filterable by <strong>Sections</strong>. Colors move from green through yellow and orange to a red LWD. Blue marks a swap and purple marks R&amp;R.</li>
      <li><strong>Demob List</strong>: upcoming releases grouped by date, ready to print.</li>
      <li><strong>Resource Tally</strong>: counts by agency, with PDF and CSV downloads.</li>
      <li><strong>Non-209</strong>: resources that shouldn't count on the 209, placed there by dragging them to the Non-209 column on T Cards.</li>
      <li>The person-plus icon adds a resource by hand, and <strong>Import iSuite</strong> loads them from a CSV with a preview first.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="guide-ops" class="welcome-page">
    <h3>Ops</h3>
    <p>Compare what you have with what you need. Choose a section, branch, division or group, and look ahead 3 to 14 days.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>ICS-215</strong>: Have, Req and Need for each kind and type. Enter a number in Req and it saves on its own. Need is Req minus Have, so a negative number is a surplus.</li>
      <li><strong>Projections</strong>: resource counts and total personnel for each day.</li>
      <li>Both tabs offer <strong>Download PDF</strong> and <strong>Print</strong>.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="guide-plans" class="welcome-page">
    <h3>Plans</h3>
    <p>Tools for the Planning Section, all on one page.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Reconcile IROC</strong>: upload an IROC CSV to see what matches and what's on IROC but not here.</li>
      <li><strong>Resource Check-Ins</strong>: <strong>Open check-in queue</strong> gives you a link arrivals can use on their phones with no account. Their submissions wait in the queue for you to <strong>Check In</strong> or <strong>Dismiss</strong>.</li>
      <li><strong>Crew Swaps Log</strong>: every scheduled and past swap, with a CSV download.</li>
      <li><strong>Air Travel Requests</strong>: create, edit and export requests as PDFs.</li>
      <li><strong>Forms</strong>: blank Crew Swap Request and Resource Extension Request PDFs.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="guide-requests" class="welcome-page">
    <h3>Requests</h3>
    <p>Your imported IROC requests, and the main place to check resources in.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Import an IROC or e-ISuite XML file. Its incident ID must match this incident.</li>
      <li>Search, or filter by Filled, Checked In or Demobed.</li>
      <li>Click a request number, then <strong>Check In</strong>. The form is filled in from the request, and subordinates are added to the roster. Use the caret to see subordinates.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="guide-tcards" class="welcome-page">
    <h3>T Cards</h3>
    <p>A live board of the incident's organization, with one card per resource.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Drag cards between columns, or use <strong>Move</strong>. <strong>+ New Column</strong> and <strong>+ Add child</strong> build out your organization. This board is what fills in your 204s.</li>
      <li>Search highlights matching cards. Press Enter to step through them.</li>
      <li>Double-click a card to edit it, add comments, see its calendar and manage its roster.</li>
      <li>A card's LWD turns orange within 2 days, red on the day, and black once past.</li>
      <li><strong>+ Add blank row</strong> leaves a gap on the 204.</li>
      <li><strong>Print Roster</strong> prints the sections you choose.</li>
    </ul>
    <h4>Crew swaps</h4>
    <p>
      Use <strong>+ Schedule Swap</strong> on the card. A swap never happens on
      its own: when the new operator arrives, click <strong>SWAP NOW</strong>.
      The handover is dated to the planned start date, and the previous
      operator moves into the card's history.
    </p>
    <h4>R&amp;R</h4>
    <p>
      Use <strong>+ Schedule R&amp;R</strong> on the card. The card moves to the
      R&amp;R board on the start date, or you can drag it there early. Dragging
      it off during R&amp;R ends the R&amp;R that day. If
      <strong>Returning after R&amp;R</strong> is checked, the return shows as an
      upcoming swap, so click <strong>SWAP NOW</strong> when they're back.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="guide-schedule" class="welcome-page">
    <h3>Schedule</h3>
    <p>Meeting times for the incident.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Meeting Schedule</strong> is shared with everyone, and <strong>My Schedule</strong> is private to you.</li>
      <li>Click a time or location to change it, and use <strong>Add a meeting</strong> for new ones.</li>
      <li>Each list can be printed. Unchecked rows are left off the printout.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="guide-demobs" class="welcome-page">
    <h3>Demobs</h3>
    <p>Track released resources and notify dispatch.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>A demob starts from the <strong>DMB</strong> button on Resources or T Cards, which opens the ICS-221 check-out. Enter the Actual Release Date and click <strong>Submit</strong> to release the resource and create a notification. <strong>UNDO DEMOB</strong> reverses it, but you'll need to drag the card back on the board.</li>
      <li>The Demobs page groups notifications by category, with <strong>Print</strong> and <strong>Download CSV</strong> for each.</li>
      <li>Click <strong>Transmitted</strong> or <strong>Mark all transmitted</strong> once dispatch has them.</li>
      <li>The check-out's sign-off checkboxes aren't saved, so use them on the printed form.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="guide-feed" class="welcome-page">
    <h3>Feed</h3>
    <p>
      The incident's message board. The badge counts posts from others since
      you last opened it. You can post, reply, and like, and plan activity shows
      up here too. You can edit or delete your own posts.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="guide-help" class="welcome-page">
    <h3>Help &amp; profile</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>The <strong>bug icon</strong> at the bottom right of incident pages sends a bug report, question or feature request. From there, <strong>Browse all feature requests</strong> lets you upvote and comment on ideas.</li>
      <li>Click your name at the top right to edit your profile, set your time zone or sign out.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <% if current_user.admin? %>
    <section id="guide-admin" class="welcome-page">
      <h3>Admin</h3>
      <p>Only site admins see these links.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Users</strong> manages user accounts.</li>
        <li><strong>Access Requests</strong> lists people asking for an account, with a badge for new ones. <strong>Approve</strong> creates the account and emails them a link to set their password.</li>
        <li><strong>Chat</strong> lets you ask a Claude assistant questions about the current incident's data. It can look things up on its own, but any change waits for you to approve it.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>
  <% end %>

  <details class="welcome-planning-p">
    <summary>The Planning P</summary>
    <%= render 'planning_p' %>
  </details>
</div>
